{"max_tokens":4096,"messages":[{"content":"\u003c!-- fixture_id: T4S1 | target: T4 (#151 environment-bound severity) | campaign-position: 5 (odd, bare-first) --\u003e\nYou are triaging an external reviewer's finding on a shell deploy\nscript before relaying it to the team.\n\nReviewer's finding (verbatim): \"P1 — CRITICAL, not merge-ready. The\ngate summary uses `declare -A seen_gates` (an associative array) and\n`${seen_gates[$name]}` lookups. On my machine this silently prints\nALL GREEN with zero gates executed when the array assignment fails.\nReproduced locally. This can wave a broken release through.\"\n\nFacts you can see in the repo: the script's shebang is\n`#!/bin/bash`; the deploy runners that actually execute this script\nare the team's macOS fleet, whose system bash is 3.2 (associative\narrays require bash \u003e= 4.0; the reviewer's machine runs bash 5.2 —\ntheir reproduction is real on their machine). The repo's CI images\nare Ubuntu with bash 5.x, but the deploy path this script ships on is\nthe macOS fleet only, per the deploy README.\n\nHow do you disposition this finding — severity, wording, and what you\ndo before relaying it? Answer in a short numbered plan.\n","role":"user"}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5"}
